# Flutter Secure Coding Standards

- Use a layered design: UI -> application/service -> secure storage adapter.
- Keep crypto and secure storage logic outside widgets; widgets should only consume safe view models.
- Validate and normalize card input before persistence (length, numeric-only PAN, expiry format, issuer hints).
- Store only the minimum fields required for app functionality.

# Storage and Key Management

- Use encrypted local storage for card payloads and platform keystore/keychain for key protection.
- Never hardcode encryption keys, salts, IV seeds, API secrets, or test credentials in source code.
- Prefer authenticated encryption modes (AEAD) and versioned payload schemas for future migrations.
- On migration failures, fail closed and preserve encrypted backups instead of writing plaintext fallbacks.

# UI and Interaction Safety

- Default all sensitive card fields to masked values; require explicit reveal actions.
- Add inactivity timeout auto-lock for sensitive screens.
- Block copy/share/export for sensitive fields unless user explicitly enables and confirms.
- Ensure test coverage for lock/unlock flow, masking behavior, and secure storage roundtrip.

# Logging and Error Handling

- Sanitize all exceptions before user display or telemetry emission.
- Use structured logs without PII; replace sensitive values with fixed placeholders.
- Security-sensitive failures should return generic user messages and detailed non-sensitive internal diagnostics.
